-- title:
--   The half-closed tile at the step index contains the point
-- statement:
--   Fix a side length $h > 0$. For $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$ let
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; \bigl(\,\operatorname{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\,\bigr)_{i} \qquad (\texttt{stepIndex d h s})$$
--   be the nearest point of $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ in each coordinate, and let
--   $$\tilde A_h(u) \;=\; \prod_{i} \bigl[\,u_i - h/2,\ u_i + h/2\,\bigr) \qquad (\texttt{halfClosedCube h u})$$
--   be the half-closed cube of side $h$ about $u$ — closed on the left and open on the right in each coordinate, so that these cubes tile $\mathbb{R}^d$ without overlap. The claim is that
--   $$s \in \tilde A_h\bigl(x_h(s)\bigr)$$
--   for every $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   This is the defining property of the step index with respect to the genuine tiling: since the half-closed cubes partition $\mathbb{R}^d$, the tile named by $x_h(s)$ is *the* tile of $s$, and $x_h$ is therefore a well-defined measurable selection of a tile for each point. Piecewise-constant approximations that assign to a point the average of a function over its tile are defined through exactly this membership.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Positivity of $h$ is assumed. The half-closed version is the one for which the tiles are pairwise disjoint; the corresponding statement for the closed cube $\{x : \|x-u\|_\infty \le h/2\}$ of the same index is separate and follows since the half-closed cube is contained in the closed one.
